  3. Reduction seed selection was lost. Reduction construction (crates/ipu-codegen/src/planner/reduction.rs:103) always seeds from contributor zero. Before
     66a9ad4b, it preferred a contributor already on the destination tile. Copy elimination cannot recover that choice once planning selects the remote
     contributor. This remains a plausible contributor to the previously reported MLP slowdown; I haven’t measured its individual impact.

• I found 20 types. The strongest objections would be to redundant state and unclear ownership—not simply to the number of small structs.

   Type                           Assessment
  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
   KernelAccess                   Questionable. Combines physical access requirements with a copied TensorFormat. The format already belongs to the bound
                                  operand’s shard. This is partly a convenience for calling kernels without the shard table.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelRequirements             Questionable organization. Its input/output lists parallel KernelRun’s input/output views, requiring repeated consistency
                                  checks. Distinct-memory-element constraints are legitimate; the duplicated operand organization deserves redesign.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelImplementation           Poorly represented. It needs to identify selected code, but mixes Exact(&str) with large positional tuples. Understanding a
                                  GEMM key requires remembering seven field positions. Named variant fields would improve this without adding types.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CopyKernel                     Redundant distinction. Its strided/contiguous variants repeat LocalCopy.pattern. Selected word width plus the existing
                                  movement descriptor should suffice.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CopyRun                        Useful role, questionable stored state. A validated copy binding is reasonable, but it stores movement, selected kernel and
                                  derived access requirements. Each retained field should justify its caching rather than merely avoiding recomputation.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   GemmWeightLoad                 Potentially redundant. Planning derives it from the right operand’s memory class, then carries it independently in the GEMM.
                                  That creates another fact which must remain consistent with operand storage.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   GemmAxes                       Mixed responsibilities. Axis correspondence describes the mid operation; valid_inner/valid_columns also carry logical bounds
                                  used for useful-work profiling. Its placement in the kernel family obscures those roles.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelAbiError, KernelError    Probably over-separated. The “ABI” error includes unsupported operations and geometry failures; the outer error mostly wraps
                                  it alongside storage/address errors. The names suggest a cleaner distinction than exists.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   OutputCapability               Useful contract, overbroad name. Currently describes a particular FP16-to-FP8 producer-fusion capability, not kernel outputs
                                  generally. I would clarify its scope rather than collapse it to today’s few constant cases.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CastChunks                     Borderline. A short-lived result containing an axis and ranges, consumed by the donation pass. The chunk computation is
                                  meaningful; the struct is optional, and should not become another persistent planning representation.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelCall                     Keep. Selected implementation plus scalar ABI arguments is a real boundary before address resolution. It is not another
                                  tensor representation.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   AttentionKernelShape           Keep, clarify naming. A named specialization key for the monolithic FlashAttention kernel, including scale bits. It is
                                  substantially more readable than the tuples elsewhere.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   RowWorkspace                   Keep. One declaration drives both workspace construction and validation. This is actual shared knowledge, not an adapter
                                  preserving two competing pathways.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   PaddingRequirement             Keep. Represents genuinely different kernel facts: padding is read, unread, or harmless under a zero-coefficient condition.
                                  Establishing the latter condition belongs to the pass.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   MemoryOperand                  Keep. Identifies an input or output in cross-operand constraints without inventing a combined indexing convention.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   AccumulationPrecision          Defensible. Restricts accumulation to supported FP16/FP32 modes. Replacing it with unrestricted Precision would permit
                                  meaningless FP8 accumulation states.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   GemmKernelMode                 Keep. Initialize versus accumulate is a real semantic distinction.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelCompilation              Keep. Describes one compilation unit: source, object name and flags.
  ─────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   KernelBuildPlan                Keep the concept; simplify its construction. It is really an object manifest and entry-point map. Most questionable
                                  complexity lies in the procedures assembling it, not these two fields.
